from typing import List

class Solution:
    def numIdenticalPairs(self, nums: List[int]) -> int:
        count = 0
        num_dict = {}

        for num in nums:
            if num in num_dict:
                count += num_dict[num]
                num_dict[num] += 1
            else:
                num_dict[num] = 1

        return count
```

This solution uses a dictionary to keep track of the number of times each number has appeared in the list so far. For each number, if it has appeared before, it adds the count of its previous appearances to the total count of good pairs, as each previous occurrence can form a good pair with the current number. Then, it increments the count of the current number in the dictionary. This approach ensures that each pair is counted exactly once and runs in O(n) time complexity, where n is the length of the input list.

