# sol = Solution()
# print(sol.selfDivisiblePermutationCount(1))  # Output: 1
# print(sol.selfDivisiblePermutationCount(2))  # Output: 1
# print(sol.selfDivisiblePermutationCount(3))  # Output: 3
```

This solution uses a backtracking approach to generate all possible permutations of the array `[1, 2, ..., n]` and checks if they are self-divisible by ensuring that `gcd(a[i], i) == 1` for every position `i`. The `used` array keeps track of which numbers have been placed in the permutation so far to avoid duplicates. The function `backtrack` is a recursive helper function that explores all permutations starting from a given position `pos`.

